# Platform Auth

This repository contains the code for the Platform Auth Nginx ingress controller.

## Building and Running

```bash
make build
make run
```

If you are running on a Kubernetes cluster that has an etcd instance already provisioned, set the `ETCD_HOST` environment variable to the hostname of your etcd instance:

```bash
export ETCD_HOST=<etcd-host>
make run
```
You can also specify any other configuration variables by setting their names as environment variables prefixed with `PLATFORM_AUTH_`. For example, if you want to customize the secret name used by the auth service, you could do this:

```bash
export PLATFORM_AUTH_SECRET_NAME=custom-secrets-name
make run
```
To use different values for the various variables in this chart, see the [values.yaml](https://github.com/pulumi/platform-auth/blob/main/values.yaml) file for more details. You'll need to install Helm v3 or later to be able to use it.

## Using with Pulumi

The following code creates an instance of the `PlatformAuth` resource and adds it to a new Pulumi program:

```typescript
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import * as k8s from "@pulumi/kubernetes";

// The namespace where the resource will live.
const namespace = "platform-auth";

// Create a new Pulumi stack.
const stack = new pulumi.StackReference(`${namespace}/stack`);

// Create a new K8s provider.
const provider = new k8s.Provider(namespace);
<|fim_suffix|><|fim_middle|>

// Deploy the Platform Auth Helm release.
//
// Note that the `version` field is optional here because we're using the default value associated with our Stack Reference.
const platformAuthVersion = await k8s.helm.v3.Chart.get("platform-auth", {
  stack,
  provider,
  version: platformAuthVersion.version,
});
```

See the [Pulumi examples repo](https://github.com/pulumi/examples/tree/master/kubernetes-helm-kubeflow) for a complete example showing how to deploy the Platform Auth Nginx ingress controller using Pulumi.

